# Pin runtime PM off on every xHCI controller. Installed to
# /etc/udev/rules.d/60-steam-machine-usb.rules.
#
# THE FAILURE THIS PREVENTS
#
# A hub error storm wedges the xHC command ring. The next runtime-suspend
# attempt then times out in xhci_suspend()'s 20 ms STS_SAVE handshake:
#
#   xhci_hcd 0000:12:00.4: Controller Save State failed -110
#   xhci_hcd 0000:12:00.4: can't suspend (hcd_pci_runtime_suspend returned -110)
#
# That sets dev->power.runtime_error, after which every runtime PM transition
# for the device returns -EINVAL forever and power/runtime_status reads "error".
# Nothing in userspace can clear it -- runtime_status is DEVICE_ATTR_RO and the
# flag only clears inside the kernel-internal __pm_runtime_set_status(). The
# bus stays degraded until the pci_dev is destroyed and re-created.
#
# A controller that never runtime-suspends never runs that handshake, so it
# cannot reach the error state. The hub still misbehaves; the controller
# survives it.
#
# WHY THE RULE AND NOT A BOOT SCRIPT
#
# The poisoning suspend happened 63 s into boot on 2026-09-05. A boot-time
# write has to win a race it does not reliably win; a udev rule applies as the
# device appears, and re-applies on the `bind` after usb-reset.sh removes and
# rescans the controller.
#
# WHY CLASS AND NOT DEVICE IDs
#
# 0x0c0330 is PCI class "USB controller, xHCI". Matching it covers all four
# functions on this board and survives renumbering -- these controllers have
# already moved once (11:00.4 -> 12:00.4), which is why hardware/audio/README.md
# still names an address that no longer exists.
#
# COST
#
# Two of the four controllers (12:00.3, 13:00.0) currently do reach `suspended`
# with nothing attached, and will now stay `active`. That is a small idle power
# increase in exchange for the bus not needing a reset to come back. Note
# system/power/ already accepts the same trade on 0f:00.0, whose USB children
# are pinned `on` for input latency.
ACTION=="add|bind", SUBSYSTEM=="pci", ATTR{class}=="0x0c0330", ATTR{power/control}="on"
